<figure class="security-map" aria-labelledby="security-map-title">
  <figcaption>
    <span class="content-kicker">Defense in depth</span>
    <h3 id="security-map-title">Put each control at the boundary it can actually see</h3>
    <p>Reachability, workload permission, and application identity are different decisions. A packet can pass NetworkPolicy and still fail TLS or authorization; an authenticated request can never arrive if routing or egress policy blocks it first.</p>
  </figcaption>

  <div class="secured-flow" aria-label="External client to protected backend flow">
    <article><small>Untrusted network</small><strong>External client</strong><code>203.0.113.5</code></article><i>→</i>
    <article class="edge"><small>Edge controls</small><strong>LB / firewall</strong><code>reachable IP + allowed port</code></article><i>→</i>
    <article class="identity"><small>L7 boundary</small><strong>Gateway</strong><code>TLS · host/path · auth integration</code></article><i>→</i>
    <article class="policy"><small>Workload boundary</small><strong>Ingress policy</strong><code>source + protocol + port</code></article><i>→</i>
    <article class="healthy"><small>Application</small><strong>Backend Pod</strong><code>identity + authorization</code></article>
  </div>

  <div class="policy-pair">
    <article><span>Source Pod</span><strong>Egress gate</strong><code>frontend → TCP 8080</code><p>If this direction is isolated, some applicable policy must allow the connection.</p></article>
    <i aria-hidden="true">→ packet must pass both →</i>
    <article><span>Destination Pod</span><strong>Ingress gate</strong><code>from frontend → TCP 8080</code><p>If this direction is isolated, destination policies must independently allow it.</p></article>
  </div>

  <div class="control-matrix">
    <article><span>NetworkPolicy</span><strong>Pod traffic at L3/L4</strong><p>Use namespace and Pod selectors for workload relationships. Use <code>ipBlock</code> mainly for external CIDRs, and verify how the deployed data plane orders NAT and policy.</p></article>
    <article><span>Gateway / proxy policy</span><strong>HTTP, TLS, and client context</strong><p>Use for hostname, path, certificate, rate-limit, and authentication decisions when the implementation supports them.</p></article>
    <article><span>Workload identity</span><strong>Who is calling, not only where from</strong><p>mTLS or signed tokens can authenticate a workload across changing addresses. Authorization remains an application or policy-layer decision.</p></article>
    <article><span>Node and cloud controls</span><strong>Infrastructure boundaries</strong><p>Security groups, host firewalls, and routing constrain Node or interface reachability. Their scope is provider- and datapath-specific.</p></article>
  </div>

  <p class="baseline"><strong>Practical baseline:</strong> confirm policy enforcement exists, default-deny ingress and egress per namespace where appropriate, explicitly allow cluster DNS and required dependencies, restrict external entry, encrypt sensitive paths, and test the effective flow from a representative Pod.</p>
</figure>

